\section*{Introduction}

The Astrobiological Cryptographic Evolution System (ACES) introduces a new
computational paradigm in which synthetic evolution is driven, constrained, and
verified by real cosmological physics while remaining fully encrypted and
zero-knowledge auditable. Traditional artificial-life systems rely on
deterministic simulation engines or pseudo-random number generators, both of
which are fundamentally predictable and vulnerable to adversarial bias. In
contrast, ACES anchors evolutionary dynamics to multi-messenger astrophysical
entropy, incorporating gravitational-wave strain, spectroscopic flux fields,
and astrometric telemetry into a homomorphic environmental driver
$\tilde{\mathbf{E}}(t)$.

This cosmological anchoring ensures that evolutionary trajectories cannot be
precomputed or manipulated by any bounded algorithm. The entropy rate of
astrophysical telemetry exceeds algorithmic compressibility limits, providing a
non-deterministic substrate that reflects genuine physical processes rather
than synthetic randomness. As a result, ACES establishes the first framework in
which digital evolution is inseparably linked to the physical universe.

ACES further advances the state of the art by enabling encrypted organisms to
evolve entirely within CKKS ciphertext space. Genomic and cognitive vectors
remain confidential throughout all evolutionary operations, yet the system
maintains full mathematical verifiability. Each state transition produces a
succinct Groth16 zero-knowledge proof, allowing external auditors to verify
correctness without accessing plaintext biological data. This combination of
confidentiality, verifiability, and physical grounding defines a new class of
zero-trust evolutionary systems.

To ensure physical consistency, ACES incorporates a unified cosmological
pipeline that evaluates ADM $(3+1)$ spacetime slices, inflationary perturbation
spectra, energy conditions, and holographic entropy bounds before any encrypted
state transition is accepted. These constraints guarantee that synthetic
evolution respects the same invariants governing real cosmological dynamics.
The result is a physically valid, cryptographically sealed, and mathematically
provable evolutionary process.

Together, these capabilities establish \textbf{Astrobiological Cryptographic
Systems (ACS)} as a new scientific discipline. ACES demonstrates that synthetic
life can evolve under real cosmological physics, remain fully encrypted, and be
verified under zero-knowledge constraints. This introduction outlines the
principles, architecture, and implications of this discovery, providing the
foundation for a unified physical--cryptographic theory of artificial
evolution.